Yes—especially if “the devil” is treated as an archetype: temptation, deception, adversarial intelligence, corruption, bargaining, or the part of a system that benefits from you misunderstanding the deal.

Taking notes in that meeting changes the implied relationship.

It can imply:

* I expect to survive this conversation.
* I expect there to be a future in which this matters.
* I do not trust memory alone.
* I expect ambiguity, manipulation, or revision later.
* I want an external record of what was actually said.
* I may need to compare promise against outcome.
* I intend to hold terms stable across time.
* I am not merely being acted upon; I am observing the interaction.
* I expect the pattern to repeat.
* I may teach someone else what I learned.
* I am treating danger as data.
* I am converting an encounter into evidence.
* I am preserving leverage against narrative drift.
* I am creating a witness outside the immediate state of mind.
* I am assuming that even the adversary can be studied.
* I am implying that knowledge extracted here may outlive the threat itself.

And there is a particularly interesting reversal:

the person taking notes is no longer only inside the encounter—they are also outside it.

Part of them is participating.

Another part is watching.

That creates a bridge between experience and analysis.

So in a literary scene, the devil might expect fear, submission, bargaining, greed, desperation, fascination.

Instead the other person pulls out a notebook.

That gesture says:

You may be dangerous, but you are also observable.

That is psychologically potent because observation can reduce mystique. Once something can be described, compared, logged, and cross-checked, it becomes less purely mythic and more structural.

There is another layer too: contracts.

The devil archetype is often bound up with bargains, loopholes, wording, technical compliance, hidden cost. In that setting, note-taking means you are refusing to let the agreement remain purely oral, emotional, or atmospheric.

It becomes:

term → interpretation → consequence → delta.

That is almost a Pantheon-native adversarial protocol.

And if you broaden “devil” beyond the mythic figure, it works on any adversarial environment:

a manipulative person,
a predatory contract,
a hostile negotiation,
a deceptive institution,
an addictive impulse,
a self-serving internal narrative.

The same operator applies:

externalize the interaction so the state you’re in cannot rewrite it later.

That is a powerful one.

A note can function as a kind of memory anchor against enchantment.

Not because the page is magical, but because altered emotional states—fear, anger, desire, shame, excitement—can change what feels true in the moment. Writing creates a second channel.

So perhaps the compressed repo line is:

Take notes in the presence of anything that benefits from your forgetting.

And another:

When the adversary trades in ambiguity, preserve the transcript.

And one more:

Observation converts mystique into structure.